xv6-x86_64, branch cpp: xv6 rebuilt in C++, one part at a time.

The main branch (tag v0.1-x86_64-c) is MIT's xv6-riscv
(https://github.com/mit-pdos/xv6-riscv) ported to 64-bit x86 PCs
that boot with UEFI, in C. This branch starts over from a boot
skeleton and brings the parts of that port back one at a time,
converted to freestanding C++20 (no exceptions, RTTI, or standard
library), keeping xv6's file and function names so the code can be
read alongside the xv6 book.

The skeleton is:

  boot/loader.c        the UEFI loader (unchanged from main)
  kernel/entry.S       sets up a stack and calls start()
  kernel/start.cpp     copies the loader's bootinfo, runs global
                       constructors, calls main()
  kernel/main.cpp      prints a line on the serial port, paints
                       the screen, and halts
  kernel/kernel.ld     links the kernel at 0x100000

and the headers bootinfo.h, types.h, param.h, memlayout.h, x86.h.
There is no file system yet, so fs.img is an empty placeholder
that keeps the loader happy.

To build and run, you need g++ and binutils for x86-64, clang and
lld (for the UEFI loader), mtools, qemu-system-x86_64, and the OVMF
UEFI firmware (Ubuntu packages: build-essential clang lld mtools
qemu-system-x86 ovmf). Then:

  make qemu            boot in qemu (CPUS=n for n CPUs)
  make vbox            boot in a VirtualBox window;
                       the serial console output goes to vbox/serial.log.
                       VBOXTYPE=headless for no window: the serial
                       console is then the unix socket vbox/serial.sock
  usb.img              a whole-disk image (GPT, one FAT32 EFI System
                       Partition holding EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI, kernel,
                       fs.img); make qemu and make vbox boot it too

To boot a real PC (64-bit, UEFI):

  1. make usb.img
  2. find the USB stick's device with lsblk (say /dev/sdX), and
     write the image; this erases the stick, so check the name:
       sudo dd if=usb.img of=/dev/sdX bs=4M conv=fsync
  3. in the PC's firmware setup, turn Secure Boot off (the loader
     is not signed); CSM/legacy boot can stay on or off
  4. boot from the stick with the one-time boot menu (often F8,
     F11, F12, or Esc)

The loader prints what it found, waits 3 seconds, and starts the
kernel, which paints the screen blue and draws "xv6". Most PCs have
no serial port, so the screen is the only output. If the loader
stops with "memory for kernel is not free", it lists the firmware's
memory map entries around the kernel's address (0x100000).
If the screen turns dark red instead of blue, the frame buffer uses
RGB order (pixel format 0); the kernel still ran.

--- the original xv6-riscv README follows ---

xv6 is a re-implementation of Dennis Ritchie's and Ken Thompson's Unix
Version 6 (v6).  xv6 loosely follows the structure and style of v6,
but is implemented for a modern RISC-V multiprocessor using ANSI C.

ACKNOWLEDGMENTS

xv6 is inspired by John Lions's Commentary on UNIX 6th Edition (Peer
to Peer Communications; ISBN: 1-57398-013-7; 1st edition (June 14,
2000)).  See also https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.1810/, which provides
pointers to on-line resources for v6.

The following people have made contributions: Russ Cox (context switching,
locking), Cliff Frey (MP), Xiao Yu (MP), Nickolai Zeldovich, and Austin
Clements.

